   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the vehicle and traffic law and the public officers law, in\nrelation to authorizing political subdivisions to establish demonstration\nprograms implementing railroad grade crossing monitoring systems by means of\nphoto devices; and providing for the repeal of such provisions upon expiration\nthereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S527",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S527-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§135-a & 1803, add §1170-a, V & T L; amd §87, Pub Off L",
    "lawSection": "Vehicle and Traffic Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S527\n\nSPONSOR: THOMAS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the vehicle and traffic law and the public officers law,\nin relation to authorizing political subdivisions to establish demon-\nstration programs implementing railroad grade crossing monitoring\nsystems by means of photo devices; and providing for the repeal of such\nprovisions upon expiration thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill would allow any local government to adopt a local law to\nimplement a program to install and operate railroad grade crossing photo\nviolation-monitoring devices at any railroad sign or signal.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends section 135-a of the vehicle and traffic\nlaw by defining a railroad grade crossing as a location where railroad\ntracks intersect a public or private highway, roadway, or sidewalk.\n\nSection 2 of the bill amends the vehicle and traffic law by adding a new\nsection 1170-a, which establishes a demonstration program imposing mone-\ntary liability on the owner of a vehicle for the failure of an operator\nto comply with section 1170 of this article regarding obedience to\nsignal to indicate the approach of a train. The liability of the owner\nshall not exceed one hundred dollars for each violation; however, the\nadjudicating authority may provide for an additional penalty not above\ntwenty-five dollars for each violation for the failure to respond to a\nnotice of liability within the prescribed time.\n\nSection 3 of the bill amends the opening paragraph of subdivision 1 of\nsection 1803 of the vehicle and traffic law, by exempting the new demon-\nstration program from certain rules governing the disposition of fines\nand forfeitures.\n\nSection 4 of the bill amends subdivision 2 of section 87 of the public\nofficers law by adding a new paragraph (q). Paragraph (q) states that\nthe photographs; microphotographs, videotapes, or other recorded images\nprepared by the railroad grade crossing photo violation-monitoring\nsystem may not be subject to FOIL requests.\n\nSection 5 provides the effective date. PURPOSE: OF AMENDMENT:  To elimi-\nnate the authorization for MTA/LIRR enforcement of the railroad grade\ncrossing violations. Enforcement authority is left to localities.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAccording to Federal Railroad Administration data, accidents at railroad\ngrade crossings have decreased over the past decade, except within the\nborders of the Empire State. Federal data indicates that from 2004-2014\nthere have been 371 collisions resulting in 63 deaths and 137 injuries.\nIn 2013 alone, there were a total of 28 accidents on the state's three\nmost prominent commuter rail lines.\n\nIn February of 2015, a northbound Metro-North Railroad passenger train\ncollided with a sports utility vehicle that had stopped on the tracks.\nThis tragic accident resulted in the deaths of five passengers and the\nvehicle's driver. While the presence of warning devices such as flashing\nlights, signs, and gates have helped to prevent such catastrophes, they\nhave not adequately deterred drivers from failing to adhere to these\ncrucial safety measures.  Additionally, on May 4, 2015, a school bus at\nRoaring Brook Road railroad crossing in Westchester County stopped only\ninches from the tracks, which left just enough clearance from the pass-\ning train to avoid an accident. However, the driver failed to leave\nenough room for the lowering gate and caused the bus to become trapped.\n\nSince March 2014, there were thirty-eight. tickets issued at this\nspecific crossing for driving violations. These near miss situations\nbring attention to the fact that aggressive or distracted drivers are\nfailing to comply with safety laws at railroad grade crossings and\nputting many in danger.\n\nThis piece of legislation would aid in the effort to decrease the inci-\ndence of vehicular accidents at railroad grade crossings by providing a\nflexible mechanism to local and regional authorities to deter driving\nviolations that result in these horrific and costly collisions. Utiliz-\ning the same technology as red light cameras, railroad grade crossing\nviolation-monitoring systems will fine drivers who fail to adhere to\nsafety signals indicating an approaching train. This fine will incentiv-\nize drivers to pay particular attention to signs indicating appropriate\nstopping distances from railroad crossings and thereby prevent serious\nand deadly accidents.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2021-22: S.252-A (Thomas) / A.7333-A (Abinanti) - Passed Senate.\n\n2019-20: S.4089 (Thomas) / A.6012 (Abinanti) - Passed Senate.\n\n2018: S.2303 (Murphy) / A.4795 (Abinanti) - Referred to Transportation.\n\n2017: S.2303 (Murphy) / A.4795 (Abinanti) - Passed Senate.\n\n2015-2016: S.5238 (Murphy) / A.7646 (Abinanti) - Passed Senate.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the state.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the thirtieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law, and it shall expire and be deemed repealed 5 years after\nsuch effective date.\n",
